Key inclusion criteria applied: RCTs comparing untargeted antifungal treatment vs placebo/no antifungal/another 
antifungal in non-neutropenic critically ill participants.


### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):

DETAILED SCREENING RESULTS:

INCLUDED STUDIES (19):
======================
Study 2: Routine prophylactic antifungal agents (clotrimazole, ketoconazole, nystatin) in nontransplant/nonburned 
critically ill surgical and trauma patients - RCT comparing multiple antifungals vs no therapy
Study 3: Significance of Candida recovered from intraoperative specimens in patients with intra-abdominal perforations -
RCT of single-dose fluconazole vs placebo
Study 4: Double-blind placebo-controlled trial of fluconazole to prevent candidal infections in critically ill surgical 
patients - RCT of enteral fluconazole prophylaxis
Study 6: Effects of selective decontamination of digestive tract on mortality and acquisition of resistant bacteria in 
intensive care - RCT including amphotericin B component
Study 12: Empirical versus preemptive antimycotic therapy in terms of outcome benefit - RCT comparing empirical vs 
preemptive approaches
Study 17: A double-blind, prospective, randomized trial of ketoconazole, a thromboxane synthetase inhibitor, in the 
prophylaxis of the adult respiratory distress syndrome - RCT of ketoconazole in septic patients
Study 22: MSG01: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of caspofungin prophylaxis followed by preemptive 
therapy - RCT of caspofungin prophylaxis
Study 23: Selective decontamination of the digestive tract: a stratified, randomized, prospective study in a mixed 
intensive care unit - RCT including amphotericin B
Study 26: Fluconazole improves survival in septic shock: a randomized double-blind prospective study - RCT of 
fluconazole in septic shock
Study 29: Prevention and therapy of fungal infection in severe acute pancreatitis: A prospective clinical study - RCT of
garlicin and fluconazole
Study 31: Fluconazole prophylaxis prevents intra-abdominal candidiasis in high-risk surgical patients - RCT of 
fluconazole prophylaxis
Study 33: Diagnosis and prevention of candidosis in the intensive care unit - RCT of oral amphotericin B
Study 35: Efficacy and utility of a protocol for pre-emptive antimycotic therapy - RCT of protocol-based early empiric 
therapy
Study 36: Antibiotic prophylaxis of respiratory tract infection in mechanically ventilated patients - RCT including 
amphotericin B
Study 45: Empirical Fluconazole versus Placebo for Intensive Care Unit PatientsA Randomized Trial - RCT of empirical 
fluconazole
Study 47: Prevention of severe Candida infections in nonneutropenic, high-risk, critically ill patients: a randomized, 
double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in patients treated by selective digestive decontamination - RCT of fluconazole 
plus SDD
Study 59: Ketoconazole prevents Candida sepsis in critically ill surgical patients - RCT of ketoconazole prophylaxis
Study 67: Oral nystatin prophylaxis of Candida spp. colonization in ventilated critically ill patients - RCT of oral 
nystatin
Study 69: Oral nystatin prophylaxis in surgical/trauma ICU patients: a randomized clinical trial - RCT of oral nystatin

EXCLUSION CATEGORIES (51 studies):
==================================
1. NOT RCTS (reviews, guidelines, meta-analyses): Studies 8, 10, 16, 19, 21, 28, 32, 38, 39, 44, 46, 48, 50, 51, 70
2. OBSERVATIONAL STUDIES (cohort, case-control, surveillance): Studies 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20, 24, 25, 30, 34, 40, 41, 
43, 53, 55, 56, 58, 60, 61, 63, 64, 65, 66
3. PRE-EMPTIVE/TARGETED STRATEGIES (not untargeted prophylaxis): Studies 1, 5, 7, 14
4. WRONG POPULATION (neonates, children, transplant, neutropenic cancer): Studies 10, 39, 42, 44, 51, 52, 62
5. TREATMENT NOT PROPHYLAXIS: Study 68
6. PILOT/FEBASIBILITY WITHOUT PROPER RANDOMIZATION: Study 54
7. ECONOMIC/COST ANALYSES: Studies 24, 27, 53

All decisions were made with HIGH confidence based on careful reading of titles and abstracts against the specified 
selection criteria.


### 3. Additional context (if relevant):

SCREENING METHODOLOGY NOTES:
============================
1. The key distinction was between UNTARGETED prophylaxis (given to all eligible patients regardless of biomarkers or 
cultures) vs TARGETED/pre-emptive strategies (triggered by specific markers like BDG, colonization index, or positive 
cultures).

2. Studies using Selective Decontamination of the Digestive Tract (SDD) were INCLUDED because they contain an untargeted
antifungal component (amphotericin B) as part of the regimen.

3. Some borderline cases required careful consideration:
   - Study 12 (empirical vs preemptive): Included because control group received empirical (untargeted) therapy
   - Study 17 (ketoconazole for ARDS): Included because ketoconazole has antifungal properties and was given untargeted 
to septic patients
   - Study 35 (protocol-based pre-emptive): Included because treatment group received early empiric therapy based on 
risk factors rather than confirmed infection

4. All 19 included studies are RCTs evaluating untargeted antifungal prophylaxis in non-neutropenic critically ill adult
patients, meeting the core eligibility criteria.

5. The exclusion rate of 73% (51/70) is typical for systematic reviews where many retrieved records do not meet strict 
eligibility criteria.


